WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
Module 2: designing the
questions about the story.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:06.531
This is where good patterns
are made, or lost.

00:00:06.531 --> 00:00:10.137
In about thirty minutes
you'll learn to write triads,

00:00:10.137 --> 00:00:15.308
sliders and stones that reveal real
tensions, and to fix a draft that won't.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.034
Each question type does one job.

00:00:18.034 --> 00:00:22.014
A triad shows the balance
between three pulls in a story.

00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:26.343
A dyad, the slider, shows where
on one dimension a story sits.

00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:30.672
Stones show how several aspects
featured, on two axes at once.

00:00:30.672 --> 00:00:34.233
And context questions let
you compare groups later.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:39.609
One more thing from the Mosaic guidance:
the template questions already work.

00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:42.193
Change them when your topic needs it,

00:00:42.193 --> 00:00:45.893
not because a form feels
like it should be filled in.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.310
Here's the test for a triad.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.404
Could someone honestly be high
on all three corners at once?

00:00:52.404 --> 00:00:56.088
If they could, the triangle
has no informative middle.

00:00:56.088 --> 00:01:00.181
So choose three pulls on one
limited thing, like a decision.

00:01:00.181 --> 00:01:04.411
The people doing the work, data
and evidence, or the deadline.

00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:09.187
Every corner should be somewhere a
real person would admit to landing.

00:01:09.187 --> 00:01:14.167
Keep labels to one or two words,
because people read them while dragging.

00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:17.237
Two things to know when
you read the results.

00:01:17.237 --> 00:01:22.149
The first label simply sits at the
top, so the top corner isn't special.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:25.424
And an empty corner is a
finding, not a failure.

00:01:25.424 --> 00:01:29.108
If nobody's story sits near
the people doing the work,

00:01:29.108 --> 00:01:31.905
that's exactly what leaders need to hear.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.132
For a slider, ask: would real people
put their stories at both ends?

00:01:37.132 --> 00:01:41.029
Keep it to one dimension, so
the middle means something.

00:01:41.029 --> 00:01:46.110
Before it was made, versus after it
was in place, is one dimension: time.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.337
Both ends must be honest places.

00:01:48.337 --> 00:01:50.494
Nobody chooses "disrespectful".

00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:56.270
Keep the ends concrete, because nobody can
place a memory against "strategic versus

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:56.966
tactical".

00:01:56.966 --> 00:02:00.933
Write them in the participant's
voice, about their story.

00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:04.900
And in exploration, neither
end should be the better one,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.240
so check that the wording
doesn't shame one end.

00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:13.720
Stones are the richest question
type and the most tiring to answer.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.154
Three to five items at most, because
completion drops sharply after five.

00:02:19.154 --> 00:02:21.014
Make every item distinct.

00:02:21.014 --> 00:02:24.736
"Leadership" and "Management"
can't be told apart.

00:02:24.736 --> 00:02:30.765
Write each axis as low end, arrow, high
end, like "Absent, arrow, Fully present".

00:02:30.765 --> 00:02:36.719
The arrow matters: SurveyCraft uses it to
tell screen-reader users where a stone

00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:37.091
sits.

00:02:37.091 --> 00:02:38.729
And one practical fix.

00:02:38.729 --> 00:02:43.269
In a standalone survey the
counter says "two of four placed",

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:49.298
which reads like a target, and nudges
people to place stones that weren't part of

00:02:49.298 --> 00:02:50.191
their story.

00:02:50.191 --> 00:02:56.146
So add a line to the description: leave
out any that weren't part of your story.

00:02:56.633 --> 00:02:59.987
Context questions are how
you compare groups later,

00:02:59.987 --> 00:03:02.683
so ask only what you'll actually compare.

00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:04.458
Keep options few and broad.

00:03:04.458 --> 00:03:08.732
Twenty-three units and three shifts
makes groups of two or three.

00:03:08.732 --> 00:03:13.334
Watch combinations, because two
filters together make a smaller group.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:14.847
And keep them optional.

00:03:14.847 --> 00:03:18.660
A good context question can
carry the client's hypothesis.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.723
Asking when someone joined, and whether
that was at Northwind or at Lakeside,

00:03:23.723 --> 00:03:27.931
tests the "different company"
story directly, with large groups.

00:03:27.931 --> 00:03:30.956
SurveyCraft hides any
group under ten stories,

00:03:30.956 --> 00:03:35.164
and you hold the same line: under
ten stories, don't discuss it.

00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:38.186
Standalone Mosaic Signal explores.

00:03:38.186 --> 00:03:43.002
Its sliders have no better end
and its triads have no bad corner.

00:03:43.002 --> 00:03:48.634
Culture Dig scores, so its questions
are given a direction: a healthier end,

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:52.413
a concerning corner, and
fixed axes for the stones.

00:03:52.413 --> 00:03:54.636
In the product's own guidance,

00:03:54.636 --> 00:04:00.860
the open instincts are right for exploration
and exactly wrong for a weighted score.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:06.491
So when a client asks for a score from
a standalone study, don't invent one.

00:04:06.491 --> 00:04:10.122
Point them to a Culture
Mosaic, or a Culture Dig.

00:04:10.633 --> 00:04:15.465
If the topic isn't covered by a template,
or the client wants an expert's eye,

00:04:15.465 --> 00:04:16.766
add framework design.

00:04:16.766 --> 00:04:20.111
A Mosaic designer tailors
the questions to your topic.

00:04:20.111 --> 00:04:25.314
Buy it before launch, because questions
shouldn't change once stories are coming in.

00:04:25.314 --> 00:04:29.155
And brief the designer with your
framing canvas, the audience,

00:04:29.155 --> 00:04:33.987
the comparisons you've promised, and
whatever earlier data prompted the study.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.620
Jamie has drafted the rest
of the decisions survey.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.822
Match each part to its main problem,

00:04:39.822 --> 00:04:43.798
then compare it with the framework
Morgan Ellis actually fielded.

00:04:43.798 --> 00:04:46.856
You'll see that framework
again in your practical.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.525
Here's your Framework Design Checklist,
covering every part of the survey.

00:04:51.525 --> 00:04:52.199
Download it.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.305
Now the module check: five
questions, four to pass.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.451
To recap.

00:04:57.451 --> 00:05:03.167
Triads need three pulls on one limited
thing, and an empty corner is a finding.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:07.219
Sliders need one concrete
dimension and two honest ends.

00:05:07.219 --> 00:05:11.199
Stones need three to five
distinct items, arrowed axes,

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:13.586
and permission to leave some out.

00:05:13.586 --> 00:05:18.000
Ask only the context questions
you'll use, with broad groups.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.519
Don't invent a score.

00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:22.341
And buy framework design before launch.

00:05:22.341 --> 00:05:24.150
Next, fielding the study.
